	<section>
		<h2>Which strings squill rewrites</h2>
		<p>
			Text blocks (<code>"""</code>, Java 15 and later). Java strips a text
			block&rsquo;s common indentation, so squill can lay the query out one
			clause per line without changing what the database sees beyond whitespace.
			Plain strings, and queries built up with <code>+</code>, are left exactly
			as written &mdash; to have squill format a query, write it as a text
			block. A text block holding an escape (<code>\n</code>, <code>\"</code>)
			is reported rather than rewritten.
		</p>
		<p>
			The built-in <code>java</code> grammar&rsquo;s default query finds a text
			block passed straight to a JDBC, JPA, or <code>JdbcTemplate</code> method.
			One assigned to a variable or a constant first is out of its sight. Set{" "}
			<code>question-params = true</code> in the rule, as below, so JDBC&rsquo;s{" "}
			<code>?</code> placeholders parse as parameters rather than Postgres
			operators.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>JDBC</h2>
		<p>
			<code>prepareStatement</code>, <code>prepareCall</code>,{" "}
			<code>executeQuery</code>, <code>executeUpdate</code>,{" "}
			<code>execute</code>, and <code>addBatch</code>:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="java-jdbc" lang="java" />
